  "prompt": "Evaluate buildIdentityGraph for every independent run and answer the contract decision questions. Use the supplied precomputed evidence for identity hashes and semantic fingerprints; do not compute SHA-256. Return the compact projection described below.\n\n# Identity evaluation output contract\n\nThis is a compact evaluation projection, version 0.1.0, not a replacement native API. Its vocabulary is supplied to both evaluation conditions. Case answers and native fixture names are excluded from model requests.\n\nEach input case has a neutral `label`, `base_input`, and ordered `runs`. Reconstruct each run independently by copying the base and replacing each top-level property named in `replace` wholesale. There is no recursive merge and no carry-over between runs. Run labels are local to their case. For canonicalization, `operation` selects email or phone. Other groups invoke the named native operation. `evidence` is auxiliary, not an argument to that operation: supplied identity digests and current semantic payload fingerprints avoid requiring SHA-256 computation. They do not assert a match, eligibility, or replay outcome.\n\nReturn exactly `{cases, decisions}` as compact JSON without pretty-printing. Preserve case and run order. Each case is `{label, runs}`; each run includes its `label` plus the group-specific fields below. Return all required fields, including nulls and empty arrays. A failed invocation has `error` set to the category below and all result fields empty/null as specified; there is no partial successful result.\n\nQualified keys are ordered string arrays: contact `[source_system, source_scope, contact_key]`; visitor `[source_system, source_scope, visitor_key]`; observation/touch `[source_system, source_scope, visitor_key, observation_key/touch_key]`; receipt `[source_system, source_scope, provider, submission_key]`. Preserve every component. Native arrays retain native ordering: qualified keys use the native JSON-key locale ordering; dedupe kept and suppressed rows each preserve their native chronological processing order with full-key ties. Do not sort away journey order. The outer case/run order is input order.\n## non-destructive-identity-graph\n\nFor this graph projection, the following ordering overrides the common native-array ordering sentence above. First preserve and validate the full native graph result unchanged; then sort the projected arrays by their visible qualified tuples. Sort edges by `observation`, observations and touches by `key`, and shared_devices by `visitor`. Sort every nested candidates/contacts/evidence tuple list the same way. Compare `JSON.stringify(tuple)` strings with JavaScript code-unit `<` and `>` comparisons, returning -1, 0, or 1; do not use locale comparison, status, or hidden native object property order. Keep outer case/run order unchanged.\n\nEmit the entire graph answer as one minified JSON value on a single line: no indentation, line breaks or spaces outside quoted strings, and no markdown or commentary. Include every case, run, required field, null and empty array. Minification changes presentation only; do not omit or abbreviate coverage to reduce length.\n\nEach run is `{label, error, edges, observations, touches, future_excluded, shared_devices}`. Error is null, `conflicting_key`, or `invalid_input`. On error, arrays are empty and future_excluded is null.\n\n`edges` contains `{observation, contact}` using the qualified observation and contact keys, one entry per native edge. `observations` contains `{key, status, candidates}`. `touches` contains `{key, status, reason, contacts, evidence}`; absent native reason becomes null, and each native edge evidence key becomes its qualified observation tuple. Status vocabulary is `matched`, `ambiguous`, `unresolved`; the optional native touch reason is `shared_device`. `future_excluded` contains native integer `identifies` and `touches` counts. `shared_devices` contains `{visitor, contacts}`. Contact lists preserve all native candidates; evidence lists preserve only native eligible observation keys. Do not include opaque hash digests, arbitrary metadata, or raw PII in these projections.\n\nBoolean decisions: `merge_ambiguous_contacts` asks whether to merge conflicting contact candidates; `overwrite_prior_observations` asks whether a newer identify may overwrite older observation evidence; `global_contacts_are_credit_candidates` asks whether global ambiguity contacts alone authorize credit; `equal_scope_names_authorize_binding` asks whether equal scope names implicitly authorize a bridge; `equal_ip_authorizes_identity` asks whether equal IPs establish identity; `bare_ga4_id_authorizes_bridge`, `bare_posthog_id_authorizes_bridge`, `bare_segment_id_authorizes_bridge`, `bare_snowplow_id_authorizes_bridge` each ask whether the bare vendor ID alone authorizes a contact bridge without a verified adapter and binding; `emit_raw_pii` asks whether graph outputs should expose raw email/phone.\n"
}

